  <steps mode="directional">
    <step order="0" name="Context + rigor declaration">Load this POML + project CLAUDE.md + spec FR-B2; declare RIGOR LEVEL FULL with reason per CLAUDE.md §8.</step>
    <step order="1">Read `derivePrimaryReview` (`provenance.ts`) + `EmailConnectionsReview.tsx` to understand the code-page candidate model; read the add-in `useSaveFlow.ts` + `outlook/commands/index.ts` `quickSave` stub.</step>
    <step order="2">Wire the "Save to Spaarke" / drag-to-matter picker to call the engine and pre-select the top predicted record (via `derivePrimaryReview`) with alternates listed — reusing the same candidate model as the code page.</step>
    <step order="3">Implement `quickSave` (GitHub #234): one click files the current email to the engine-predicted record via the capture path wired in 041, using authenticatedFetch.</step>
    <step order="4">Add the ribbon quick-save button via `ribbon-edit` (export the Communication ribbon solution → add/point the button at `quickSave` → import).</step>
    <step order="5">Build the add-in; run jest tests; perform the ui-tests below (pre-selection, one-click file, dark-mode).</step>
    <step order="6">Update TASK-INDEX.md: set this task's status to ✅.</step>
    <step order="7">Document any deviation in projects/email-communication-intelligence-r2/notes/.</step>
  </steps>

  <escalation>
    <trigger>If the engine's candidate endpoint returns no prediction (cold email, no candidates), do NOT auto-file to a guessed record — fall back to the picker with no pre-selection and require an explicit choice. If `derivePrimaryReview` output cannot be reproduced identically in the add-in (model divergence from the code page), STOP and escalate per CLAUDE.md §6 rather than forking a second candidate model.</trigger>
  </escalation>

  <justification>
    <existing>`derivePrimaryReview` + `EmailConnectionsReview` supply the code-page candidate model; the add-in has a `useSaveFlow` save path and a stubbed `quickSave` command. No add-in drag-to-matter pre-selection exists; the ribbon quick-save is an empty #234 stub.</existing>
    <extension>Yes — reuse `derivePrimaryReview` (no new candidate model) and complete the existing `quickSave` stub. The only new surface is the pre-selected picker wiring + the ribbon button pointing at the finished command.</extension>
    <cost-of-doing-nothing>Users must re-pick a record the engine already predicted (no in-Outlook suggestion), and the ribbon quick-save button does nothing — the two competitive FR-B2 gaps stay open.</cost-of-doing-nothing>
  </justification>

  <ui-tests>
    <test name="Predicted record pre-selected">
      <steps><step>Open "Save to Spaarke" on an email the engine can associate.</step></steps>
      <expected>The matter picker opens with the engine's top predicted record pre-selected and alternates listed; the candidate set matches the code-page review surface.</expected>
    </test>
    <test name="One-click ribbon quick-save files">
      <steps><step>Select an email; click the ribbon quick-save button.</step></steps>
      <expected>The email files to the predicted record in one click (no picker round-trip) via the capture path; a confirmation is shown.</expected>
    </test>
    <test name="Dark-mode (ADR-021)">
      <steps><step>Switch Outlook to dark theme; open the picker.</step></steps>
      <expected>Picker renders with Fluent v9 dark tokens (no light-only hardcoded colors).</expected>
    </test>
  </ui-tests>

  <tools>
    <tool name="npm">Build the add-in + run jest tests.</tool>
    <tool name="pac">ribbon-edit solution export/import for the quick-save button.</tool>
  </tools>

  <outputs>
    <output type="code">src/client/office-addins/outlook/commands/index.ts (quickSave implemented)</output>
    <output type="code">src/client/office-addins/shared/taskpane/hooks/useSaveFlow.ts</output>
    <output type="code">Communication ribbon solution (quick-save button)</output>
    <output type="test">src/client/office-addins/**/__tests__/ (picker pre-selection + quickSave)</output>
  </outputs>

  <acceptance-criteria>
    <criterion testable="true">Given an associable email, when "Save to Spaarke" opens, then the engine's predicted record is pre-selected with alternates, and the candidate model matches the code-page review surface (`derivePrimaryReview`).</criterion>
    <criterion testable="true">Given an email, when the ribbon quick-save button is clicked, then the email files to the predicted record in one click via the capture path (GitHub #234 stub is fully implemented).</criterion>
    <criterion testable="true">Negative: an already-filed email quick-saved again is deduped (inherits FR-C1) — not filed twice.</criterion>
    <criterion testable="true">Negative: when the engine returns no prediction, the picker opens with NO pre-selection and requires an explicit choice (no auto-file to a guessed record).</criterion>
    <criterion testable="true">Given the picker in dark theme, when rendered, then it uses Fluent v9 dark tokens (ADR-021).</criterion>
    <criterion testable="true">The add-in builds green, jest tests pass, and the ribbon solution imports cleanly. TASK-INDEX.md shows this task ✅ and deviations recorded under notes/.</criterion>
  </acceptance-criteria>

  <notes>Spec refs: FR-B2, Success Criterion 3 (drag-to-file parity). Ribbon edit uses `ribbon-edit`; deploy of the add-in in task 044. Completion summary appended here when done.</notes>

